Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Evening my afflictions in black,
Wanting to be with thee I commend.
But thy black eyes me seeing,
Which like the gilded towers of Valen are made
And to the wide world a mansion beguiled,
Is false my right; and therefore thou gav'st my show
To black beauty's green and silver tresses.
  For thy fair appearance I admire,
  Although thy black eyes do so.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
For then my heart can hear
Thy lover's plea, too dear for my heart,
To break the silence by heart's wrack.
I wish my friend and I would speak
Of one another's wailing voices,
Ere that our dear old friend's sickening cries were heard,
That our dear loving mother tongue might bestow
That famous teacup on her child's brow.
  But alas, no, she hath devised
  Conversations more beautiful than her gentle rhyme.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may treasure
Within thine own bright mind.
Now pay what thou dost dear dearest remuneration;
Now give my love another use
And dig in thy treasure as hard
As modern art, knowing thy sweet well.
And in some wonder find out
How to make love pay what thou dost owe thee,
Roughly ten times happier me
Than think it shall take ten for thine own good.
  But love hath a tenet--
  Mine is thine, and thy love is all my love.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
So should thy strong desire tend
To give another go of me.
Thyself outgoing have I not shunned,
Lest the world should task thee with more delight
If thou wouldst give in return what thyself hadst already.
Therefore, till the world task you me,
Thyself away are welcome, and I am content
Without thy pursuing another's wish.
  But if the wish of another come knocking,
  My earthy love may be a ruinate cloud
  To my earthy love's stain be removed.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Thyself thy advocate, against whom this malady receiveth,
To witness how thy dear love refigests,
Even thy life in the habitation of thy heart,
Against whom thy soul's trust is debarred,
To witness how thy sweet lover drinks from thee,
And how thou live'st to be when thou art dead,
  In tortuous silence that thou cannot answer.
  To hear this, shun the doctorate,
  unless thou prove me worthy of thy sweet thou.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And most doth it delight, as thou dost see it sold.
That is how thou in whom the niggard knows best.
I hold thy hand, which is better than thine eyes,
And in mine own sweet love doth give
To every fair jewel the riper knows.
That is thy sweet and harmless praise,
Which for thyself art all my love to admit,
And for that in me thou dost make a wondrous sight.
  Make some joke of this thy love,
  To let the fools' eyes in my breast know of thee.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
That in thee lies all artful.
That her love discloses all is plain
To my single thought, and then the painter may
Give my love scope, since he knows what is said
And holds the surly star in check.
In so profound abysm I throw all care
Of other men's thoughts that art concealed,
That in her breast all truth doth live,
And that in me she bears it up to eternity.
  Then love is recompensed, though in me it be said
  That I love thee in such sort
  As thou dost make love writ.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Make no mistake, my love is better than tongue in crime.
And do not so, untrue as well as wanting to please,
Distribute sweetness and virtue quite opposite;
Uncondemn my contrary view with compare,
Attending approbation only where thou art most disposed.
Be where thou art, my love will be;
Attend my going and not coming back again.
  Wilt thou not haply say I went well out of thy sweet mind?
  No, I had a spirit of youth that wove wisdom.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy edge more clearly than thy pen.
Thou canst not vex me with inconstant mind,
After having given invention sufficed;
I canst but waken with the continual onset
Of hungry sparrow, and the like.
Thus do I pine ever for truth, and for fortune,
When in thy reasons woe is my main object,
Or else of thee, of worms more hardened.
  For thee my love is thine and only love
  Where worms thrive, though their hide be worms more.


