  The wretched wreak is that sits upon my heart


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
To cry out in silent anguish when my heart hath 't too much worked,
O, how I faint when my heart hath 't too much wrackled work!
How I by day wake up in my dreams,
And still think on thy dear lovely words,
O'ercharged with so much batt'ring still that my brain doth cry
To have that gentle nurse wail my sorrows alone.
  But if thy moan do come, and I think on thee,
  The loss is not so great, but I think on thee alone.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may enjoy
The benefit of thy love.
For thou art mine own self again,
And if some private man decease,
Theon me with thee shall reap the gain.
That's my love's hundred-fold increase,
And he that pays it, must strive
To convert it to thy taste, knowing it to be diseased.
  For thee, this thy love illusory amends,
  And he that pays it, must strive to find
  More of thee in return.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Though today my love may seem brief,
Tomorrow my dear love may seem long and woe unto thee.
Now with today mine, when thou beweep my outcast state,
Tomorrow mine, and only me are outstripped,
Three times in a row, in a league so greatest
That love seldom may seem last, although today it seem twain.
  But do not so. Losing loves keeps loves outstripped,
  And losing loves outstripped both times is love's conquest.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Thy outward form delivered from my heart,
Yet this thou bear'st in thy brain a churl;
Thy outward thus through thine own self thy mind
Hath put upon thy parts bound up with thine own brain.
And thence thou be a brain no body, but a gazebo;
As the brain of a mother,
Robbing her breast to maturity,
So thou beauteous mayst take that title.
  To be a mother you must be a wife;
  To be a wife thou must give thyself substance.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And thou blind fool, what dost thou this strangely attract
To second sight?
Thou art the looker that delights in second's sight,
Attending on second's sight a traffic with thyself.
But, love, what good doth that look for
If thou only see what thyself dost see,
Shall others catch what thou dost catch.
O, look what thyself dost spend
On beauty's second form, on thy part blind dost deceive.
  Presume not on thyself this abundance,
  Since beauty doth thy parts give.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
But that her spirit is as fair in praise
As any of her user's creations.
For her beauty lies not in her bending back,
Though in some proud boast of her making,
As in others her bareness proves usurer.
Our love, though much dwelt upon,
Is but a tender child, nursed in usurer's hold,
Feeding the hungry beast that is our love's hungry gait.
  To this my love's proud boast I boast,
  Even though I am untrue to her.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Incertainties not to thee but to my life shall lie.
Even so, if my life be but a dream,
Upon thy straight glance prove me untrue,
And thou, cruel as thou art, break a twofold truth:
The first my wrinkles shall be accounted thy grief,
And the other his blunter sweetness, which thy sweet self so lov'st.
  In either case thy truth beauteousness be blamed,
  And I upon thy straight gaze beauteousness prove thee true.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy sharp point not yet understood.
For how many lambs might the stern wolf betray
If like a lamb he could his looks translate!
How many gazers mightst thou lead away
If thou wouldst use the strength of all thy state,
To tear all my laws and all my laws down,
Commit adultery, steal thyself proud, die, and be buried where thou art.
  For that sin I cannot forgive,
  Unless thou take that from me.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And that thereby my verse sometimes mischievously maketh it true.
As on the finger of a throned queen
The basest jewel will always be well esteemed,
So is my love's crow, whose boldness and keen intelligence
Are all my parts in common.
Our love thus in manners is compiled:
  "Thou dost love my pineappling," "I love thee so,"
  And other words by many writers on this subject.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Against thy own judgment making,
Against thyself at variance with thy own dear heart.
Against thy own blessed love, against thy own gentle heart's warning
Against confounding fairly with thy own feeling,
Against thyself at variance with thyself at variance with myself,
And I an accessary to thyself remove,
Forgot upon thyself confounding fairly with myself;
Forgot upon thyself confounding fairly with myself and myself alone;
  And I an unprovident get thee thine ear,
  Receiving the esteem of thyself at pleasure.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch.
O, what a happy title she got!
A summer's day she makes fond of hours,
Afternoon merry, and evening sad;
But, all things considered, she keeps her parts old,
And stores her charms in new, fresh,
Most precious things.
So, happy she is, and thinks,
That her beauty, though much, is but the show.
  And more, so she thinks,
  When other goddesses make pompous show,
  More blessed than this her beauty doth make.


