Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Whose white garments with their eyes I view proudly.
Yet well they know better than I the transgression
Of thy beauty do mock,
Which on my black eye methinks is thine,
For 'twixt thy graces and thy looks I view smiling.
And yet they that I in their black garments view,
Do in their judgment reckon up my condition;
I am half dead, and they mock me more
Than they that on thy fair cheek I view smiling.
  For I am wretched and they mock me less
  Because I do not love them who mock me.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
Since then my well-contented moan alone shall make
My every wrinkle suborned,
That my life in thee lies.
Ah, but that's not enough; my tears need a foundation,
Be they festering or healing?
Lean penury within that pen doth dwell
That to his subject lends not enough his graces.
Even so, shames God the Father that 'gainst my frailties
To give any more than is due me.
  So, pity him that gave life and so deserves my death.


