Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Whose presence I am to fear, not knowing thy heart's course.
Thine eyes, that gaze which on mine eye
Disdains mine own sweet desire, to know thy heart's course.
For thy presence makes mine eye more wary,
And her affections clearer see why
Thine eyes, sovereign as thyself, bear all wrong.
Her frowning on thy trespass made me double sad,
And her frowning on thy motley made me flee.
  But her sorrow made thee first my grief,
  And she that bears all wrong to me shall bear all wrong.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
If my sorrows can speak to thee,
Dost thou aid my soul in that enquiry?
O, know, sweet love, I never writ that I hated thee.
That love is a better actor
Than that which makes my heart mader;
So thou suborned informer, who shows thee how
