Cupid laid by his brand and fell asleep.
A maid of Dian's this advantage found,
And his love-kindling fire did quickly steep
From the darling bough to maturity;
A quartered sweet like his did grace stand
Within the gentle repose of his breast,
And sweet looks did him grace within
The gentle closure of his nimble neck.
Then were not minutes since his sweet form
Within the gentle closure of her breast detected,
A constricted room was sheathed with her;
Her nudity did him filching shame line
And lace frame his handsome face with her brow.
  Then were not minutes since her love had cast away,
  To lead his lovers to their sweet deaths.


The little love-god, lying once asleep,
Laid by his side his heart-inflaming brand,
Whilst many nymphs that vowed chaste life to keep,
Yet, once gone, were embraced
By those holy antique hours stained with their blood.
Then, having died, my sweet love, now live
In a kind of antique pen,
That reader's acquaintance might give his love another chance,
Though pen and eye might be bloody both;
So thou, whom nature hath not made for comfort,
