With what I most enjoy contented least;
Yet in these thoughts myself almost despising,
Haply I think on thee, and then my state,
Like to the lark at break of day arising
From sullen earth, sings hymns at heaven's gate;
 


O me, what eyes hath love put in my head,
Which have no correspondence with true sight!
Or if they have, where is my judgment fled,
That they in my bosom's shop should make no summer of another's green,
Orchard or tall building with tanned antiquity.
Nor can I fortune to brief minutes tell,
Pointing to each his thunder, rain, and wind,
Or say with princes if it shall go well
By oft predict that I in heaven find.
But if some suspect of ill masked not thy show,
That thou among the wastes of time shouldst depart,
Leaving me nothing more to woe.
  Then should I be contented
  Where thou art not, to show my love untrue.


Canst thou, O cruel, say I love thee not
When I against myself with thee partake?
Do I not think on thee when I forgot thee,
And yet love not much more nearly
Than niggard truth would willingly impart.
I grant, sweet love, thou art much too fair
To be pigeon-tricked, much too fair
For myself to be praised of others' seeing.
But that is not so my praise
To make of others' false compare.
I may not evermore acknowledge thee,
Lest my bewailed guilt should do thee shame,
