Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
While thy spirit still praises me in thy parts.
Thou art the fairest in heart, which as a tomb
Worthy obsequious obsequies
To thee I solemnly place,
Since thou art so dearly loved here.
That is why I mourn, and that is why I weep;
To love offenders I give such a pain,
That I by mine own contrariety tend
To make mine outward love worse.
  Then, to love offenders, I give a part,
  To love thee thine only I bring in my part.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
To hear my friend's and myself's cries,
Even to tears that well in my heart
Th' inviting of sad tears to weep?
O, no, it is more; yet more is not so.
It is worse to be loved than hated,
More to suffer than to be made happy;
And more is not so
Unless it be swallowed up in sorrow.
  Lascivious sorrows make th' inviting
  More bitter than sweet tears.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May bear the rent of thine equally.
Therefore shall I live, like one more dearly in debt
 Than both his high deserts, to thee
Or than one less loved in thine eye,
For to give another's debt another's praise
Or else to be self-willed
To do thy will, evermore bending
To the like desert which thou art.
  For to thyself I'll yield nothing;
  But thou must give me back my treasure too, my true love.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Whilst that thou alone call'st me good;
Till at length, though thy name no longer exists,
Thou mak'st living a better name
By living as thou term'st good.
Thou that art so fond on compliments,
Most worthy seem'st thou my tarrying here,
When thou art as idle as a tomb,
Feed'st thyself to thy heart's content
And nothing more to thee than thy content.
  But if that be thy content, then love, love, stay.
  Ere that thou thyself depart, do not


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Now, if thou haply the least part of mine eye
Want'st thy love, know thyself to be gone;
In losing that mine eye, in losing thy soul,
In losing both thy comfort and thy well being,
In losing both thy good and thy evil part,
In losing both thy blessing and thy curse,
In losing both thy friends and thy foes,
In losing both thy blessings and thy curse,
In losing both thy dear and thy dear woe,
In losing both thy dear and thy dear hate,
  And in losing both thy parts in this woe.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
But that thou, mine, art more gross
Than at a peepstone's height.
How art thou so gross, so dark, so dost thou use
So great a store of precious light?
Mine eyes, like those of thy mother's day,
Do not like the clear to be dyed
Or the deep vermilion mixed with the day;
They look on thee as thou goest
And, soft of heart, trust in thine;
  Me, of myself I curse,
  For bending all things to thine eye.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Whose bad conduct no man's face may set;
And though my thoughts, in their zealous pilgrimage,
Upon that bier shake,
Resembling brass, nor wood nor stone,
A wonder of the world's not;
Nay, even in the grave, there is found
That heavy Saturnian repose,
Which wriggles with itself to lie down.
  This repose brings me much grief,
  For to see her bent over that jiggles is not sweet.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Tanner not my beauty with false painting,
But straight mine own cheek to truth tell;
Him in thyself clean shaven should I live,
Whose unprovoked transgression would stain my love
More bright than thy painted counterfeit.
O, know, sweet love, thy worth never falls
To the level of a painted counterfeit;
For thou art still the fairest that heaven can make,
Even so far as my unperfectioned love doth aspire.
  In striving after that which is not,
  I honor thy worth, even so thy name doth give.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy deeds more than thy deeds show.
O, know, my love, thy worth is greater
Than any blot on the map of thy days.
Though thou repent, yet I fear not
The weight of a penitent wretch'ry heart;
On the contrary, I commend
The worth of thy worth, which in thy actions doth lie.
  Then, when thou art grown and come of age,
  The world will be thy wat'ry clerk, and I no longer write.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And in thy breast doth live
That which in mine eye doth lie.
Thou canst not love those eyes that mine eye doth see,
For they in thee mend a thousand abuses.
O, what wretched errors hath thy heart committed
In loving thine eyes so dearly,
When mine eyes did adore thy fair face so well?
  Thy love was new to me, and then but in my verse,
  When mine eyes were new to thy dear heart.


