On that of others, more just, fairer,
More chaste, more true, more worthy.
Thou canst not, love, make war upon my amorous moan,
To groan afresh when thou reviewest
The bliss of past wrongs brought on thee.
Love canst not make war on my amorous moan,
To mourn for ills that do not exist,
To excuse thy trespass when thou reviewest
The bliss brought about by faults past done.
  O, change thy perspective, sour as thou wilt be,


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
To the wood, or at least so it flies in the wind.
But lo, one of those winged lambs,
With straightened hind legs and huge eyes,
Strikes her with his wing and her in a rage
Crawls her down to earth, where she parts for show,
The wat'ry wat'ry wat'ry beauteous brood
To pieces her for a view ugly.
  Thus do I now see thee, my love, whilst thou art
  Walking on the ground, which makes me sad.


