Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
An eye to heart that sheds no light
Of love's face towards me. But thou hast removed my
Darkness, my love, and put me in this;
Darkness hath put me in the knowledge
Of mine own shadow, and I can find
That it is not thy love to me,
But that I am the decease of many.
  Since then, thou gav'st me acquaintance
  Where mine own shadow doth live, to give light.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
To linger on that which I do hate so,
For thinking on that brings me no joy?
O, what can be more wretched than to be loved loved
And to have that love lodged in my heart
Which on that love doth depend
But that I may grind my teeth and weep
Much longer than is necessary?
Mark how I would be dulled were I not loved
Nor spurned on nor looked upon
As some worthless flower doth put on sport;
  For were I to grow fond of that love,
  My life would be a continual grief.


