In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And, in that love, they measure by errors.
For if they do love thee, much more doth my heart love thee
Than when I, that reviewer, make comments on thy show.
Mark how with this I begin to love thee again,
When, for the love of thee, I review
The errors that in myself I research;
And, in my thoughts, better judgment commits.
  Such is the progress of errors, that, in looking on myself
  As myself revieweth myself, so I review thee.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
And hate's counterpart hate doth steal sweet hours from love's delight.
To thee mine own love lends as a poor beggar doth his wealth,
And hate's loving-kindness steals from love's treasure.
Therefore mine own love hath a more wretched aspect
Than that of the rich whereof he's the deceiver.
Thou art as the hungry crow that heaven's sweetest may catch,
A vassal bound to the winds of change,
Who with the bait hovers in hope of some favor,
But whose hope is thine, and hate the better part.



Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
To the distance where she loves to be.
The dove, which for fear of death robs her dear
Of both body and breast, so falls to death;
But the lion fears no such fate,
Nor breaks his paws to pluck the babe from her.
The great feline fears but her beauty's youth,
Since young lambs and strong foals do prove
At such trimming that he no more fears
Of their accidents than if he had seen
Before. Yet love knows him fairest when from thee
Fair lambs flee, since she by no means doth change.
 


