Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
One in my thoughts, another in my deeds;
For why love's face so black I cannot guess,
Or what that unhappily gives thee.
Thou art blamed for my woe, and for my life
As publicans are blamed for their manners;
Yet, proud as I am of that well,
My bones and blood would prove thine if it were me.
  Hence, 'tis love that I keep quiet,
  Not thinking on thy show, fearing to be false to thee.


