Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
One on another's neck,
One on his breast, another on his waist,
Three in all, and in every part,
Resembling grieved and intented mourners;
Yet in the judgment of all these they were wrong,
For thou gav'st them power to dress such apparel
As suited their rank and condition.
O, why, therefore, do I scorn to change my look,
Not to give lame hope to thine eyes,
But to be self-substantial and to be o'erawed,
  Being at a loss what to say to myself.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
That I through mine own weakness must bow,
Looking on that worth which thou dost give?
O, more, much more, than that wound doth seem,
For that I must attend on it still,
Attending on thy worth with mine own heart.
For to love another I must bow,
And to love thee I must bow,
Just to that love which thou dost give.
  For thee, and for myself so find,
  That our dear love may never be taken from us.


