Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
To the place where thou art disposed to stay.
Yet in myself I feel most woe
When away from thee, from myself I faint;
For who can so fair be beloved
Whilst inwardly so foul is committed?
Thou art the fairest jewel in my eye,
Which to my true love's eyes doth lie,
And in myself I feel most ill,
As from myself I flee, to love someone else.
  To love is a defect in me,
  And to love I leave it to be remedied.


