By knowing thy true colors from my falsehood;
  And thou in me thine own estate,
  When I am dead and buried in thy sweet self.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And therefore their diamonds are dull,
Thine eyes are the windows to heaven.
For why should thy glass more bright be mine,
Than those of my fairest breast, which are thine?
Or why should mine own bright be thine,
Since thou dost common good use give
And set a fairer example than my painted counterfeit?
No, neither; for thou art much too fair
To be my jewel, much less to be my glass.
  To give thy glass back again is my love's revenge.


