Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
One on another's neck, to show their grief;
Yet in these signs their sorrow seemed to me
Much more sorrow than their wailing could make plain.
Thine eyes, that taught me to be woe
Even to the edge of woe, yet taught me to be kind,
And held out their hand against my woe;
Yet in these signs their grief seemed to me
Much more grief than their wailing could make plain.
  Yet in these signs their woe seemed to me
  Compared with their wailing they seemed woe;
  So I put thee in my heart


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
To think on that which gives thee such grief?
Even so my friend should be hanged for my crime,
For that same heart that so loved me so doth now depart,
Dulling my lines and doing me disgrace.
Shall I not then be wailed, like a third wife,
For that which gives thee so much grief doth give
As both my friend and I to be alone;
  And, like her, bare root, like her giveth grief to see.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may pay
The full cost of what I will vow not to renew.
But, love, think of that which shall be:
The earth can yield me but a common grave,
When thou art entombed in the world,
And my legal papers there remain untouched
That to time and posterity may make another tomb.
  But that is to be devised, and that is to be done soon.


