And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Whereto thy jealousy hath my leave to give,
And, spite of thee, I will fight thee till my blood
Comes out red with spurring blood.
That right is thine, and that right well shall stand,
Though thou be wracked and heavily batt'ring me;
I will not acknowledge it unless thou prove
How oft my life hath been the prey of robbers,
And by that proved tyrant best acquainted.
  Lascivious grace, in whom all ill well shows,
  Kill me with spites, yet I will not haply defend thee.


