They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
But that thou, being most rich in knowledge,
Most worthily preposterously dispraise
The very term beauty that makes for thy show.
For to mine eyes thou hast added another respect,
Stretching out the truth farther than the dumb can leap,
Making a fool of love, in whom all kinds of falsehood meet.
  Mine eyes, 'gainst thy sharp invention, are thine;
  And thine are thine even in thy dumb rage.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Who for his love hath no knowledge of truth,
But loves only those that truth doth publish.
In other words, she loves what truth doth publish
More than she knows how to do good by;
And for this she is blamed even by my lovers' eyes,
Whereto she hath no charter from me.
  Yet so they think, in their hearts at least,
  That she by this shall live in eternal shame.


