Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Have not brought me here to sell thy fame,
But fenced me in with thy love,
So that my trespass shall not be remembered
Save that thy love hath put this in my mind:
That this poor wretch, to be sold,
Shall I not haply call thee, though yet I love thee;
  For thy love, though yet I hate thee,
  Shall in my mind love be well remembered.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
