Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
For thy sweet self would make black uniforms new;
But since my needing is so great,
Thou deserv'st not black, but fair in my sight.
Therefore I request thee this favor,
That thou mayst through me omit
The making of false introductions,
That I may not be obsequious
To inferior beings, being thy equal,
When in inferior rank I naturally sit.
  This shall be done, and I will not complain
  ; love, be thy servant ever.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
To bear the cross of love alone,
And to be hated of many, yet love's seal kept?
O, 'tis enough that my friend suffer,
For I by myself must be hated,
And by many give my friend's name but shame.
Shall hate be more extreme than love's sharp eye?
Be as my friend were with me, and yet I must ne'er love you
Unless you were my true love;
Which being false, dear friend, I will be false ever.
  I will be your false friend, though I never love you.


