One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
To the rearward of her pride.
Another calls to her from the trees,
The third from the herd,
And still another calls to her from far off,
Still praising her beauty, despite her skill.
One by one all the calls stopped,
But still the proud mother cried out to her proudly,
"Now I see thee, now I know thou art not child
And therefore to haste is impaneled
A journey of many a day to and from thee."
  She thus endured, happy in knowing it was
  But a child, that her wailing could not be so loud


