Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
To myself alone, where thou art not.
For why should I then be black,
When thou art all beautified in my sight,
And there art none else like me but thou art?
Thou art thy mother's eyes, and they made thee so;
And yet they loved not thy beauty,
Kissing at thy cheek, as if it knew not thy heart;
And, to make them more fond of thee,
They traveled thither, and there looked on thee
Not so much as thy black brow above their breath;
  But, by heaven, they loved it so,


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
To bear with my woe alone,
When I am able to cry out to thee,
Even from the deepest wound, to the furthest farthest?
O, no, it is far worse than that.
For I can bear it no more alone,
Within the knowledge of mine own deepest grief,
Save that I know thee bring me light.
  Then, if I lose thee, my loss is my love's gain,
  And loss is love's love's joy.


