  Then thou hast reasons to be happy;
  But I have none, and thou mayst prove none.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
More than enough to make thee wretched,
And more than enough to make me forget thee,
When other minds may give thee pleasure.
Thou canst not, love, double-check thyself;
For what's so great is it which makes thee happy,
Or at least so large a part of it doth lie.
O, what a mansion have those happy hours got
Which for themselves chose not to share!
  All households have their gardens, and in them live
  Beneficiaries, many happy and many sad.


