One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
To the rearward of her fledglings.
The rest of her race follows her,
And, all alone, she forwards her pace,
Goring no pace, being self-doing.
Then, if her race be gathered,
Her beauty divided, her state divided,
Her laws observed, and her behavior predicted.
  But, for she is not alone,
  Some strong wind smothers her out, and, all alone,
  She robs her children of their dress, and lays waste their mother.


