Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
One heart in another's bosom desiring change.
How much more praise deserved thy sweet self-love
If thou couldst answer "This fair child of mine
Shall sum my count and make my old excuse,"
Proving his beauty by succession thine.
If some mother conceived such a conceit in thee,
As to give birth to a son fair,
She shouldst be stol'n, like a wife's child,
Or husband's son, to stop the flow;
But since she wrought not such a thing,
Her praise is misplaced, and she too wrong.



Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
To bear the cross of love's great shame,
When my friend and I are one?
O, no; but yet I feel I must be true,
Even to the edge of love's blunting knife,
That I cannot lose either friend or acquaintance
Without making myself a worse wretch.
So I keep my friend's eyes on me,
And then dare not look into their souls;
  Seeking that help which my heart cannot bear.


