One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch.
Another, perhaps, more gentle, keeps her from being
Ran away to breed another,
A third, to stay her from being
A hubby to his mistress' lovely son;
And so on, each in his way,
Stealing till he hath got what is best.
But thou, all in war with Nature for dominion,
Stirred by your fancy, to strive to be what thou deserv'st.
  To this I witness call the fools of time,
  Which die for goodness who have lived for crime.


