One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch.
With warning calls she calls to her brood,
But no sound from her where she strives;
Then, winged, she flies with all speed to
The bait of her mates, whose keen eyes
Doth perusal what prey doth see.
One such churl, though mounted on her mother's back,
Strikes with such force that her wing doth sway,
Bearing the mate of her fledging breed,
Which looks pale, but well hath it this skill:
  To love from afar doth prepare.


