Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
To make my melancholy more general.
And for this they were negligent,
Not knowing thy love to be well understood,
Unlooked for joy in thy things that joy brings;
For blunting the fine point of difference,
They did not perceive thy changing mood,
Which they esteemed as mutual affability.
  Thus, sad and alone, I love them still,
  But then, no longer sad, I send them away.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
To bear my friend's grief alone,
And yet seem to him my very being wail?
O, let me suffer, though more alone suffer,
With eyes more bright than his blackened blood;
Then love's sweet sorrow can give substance
To that heart that gives my friend so much grief.
Look what wealth my friend hath,
And yet suffers alone to feel it;
That sorrow hath riches enough to make him weep
Much more than spite from me alone.
  But if my friend's sorrow bring on me
  Then weep I will cease to be, and love no more.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine heir may have
That right of succession which is so dear
To me alone as thy love doth belong.
Therefore have I frequent been with strangers,
And given to the thought of new acquaintance
Thy sweet self to call home.
Now come and take possession of that right
Which is so dear that I in my will
Have no other than to give it back again.
  Such is my love, to thee I so belong,
  That, so much in love, I can live no other.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Than that which in thee doth lie.
That due is due to thee which thou deserv'st alone,
And that due to none but thine alone doth give.
In so fair a house as mine is,
The ambrosial odor of summer's rose
Doth make my love more strong, more sweet, more strongly
For that it doth in me here live.
  But if thou live remembered not to be,
  Die single, and thine image dies with thee.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
If thou wilt deny this, know that I have recourse
Against thy very heart, though thou art untrue to me.
I may detain time, but not eternity,
And keep an audit of thine unbroken right.
So thou repentest of my love,
And my appeal is this, that thou wilt love me still
While I, against thy willful assent,
Do nothing whatsoever to change my will;
  Save that thou promise me to love thee still,
  When thou art old and me unyielding grow.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
But do not the like unto it in their thinking.
For, behold, it is within thine own heart
That doth share in thy soul's substance;
That is thyself, that dost love it here.
Now, what do thy heart's flowers desire
Unless they see within thy glass,
Which heavy censer doth play thine own sweet part?
  Thy glass will show thee how thy glass doth look,
  If thou wilt look in it. Then, love, be thou the better part of me.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Who for his love hath no respect to beauty set;
And having such a father as her husband doth lie,
I swear by heaven my love is his.
But when my love for truth finds a wrinkle
Like a false friend's carved likeness,
Mine eye is deceived, and my love is deceived.
  For to no beauty hath my love dedicated
  Save that which in him doth live.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Have I not said enough to thee?
Be where thou art, my love, and I will excuse
The rudeness of thy haste,
Knowing thy worth and thy reasons.
Make answer, love; I love not to hear
That thou forget'st thyself so high.
O, what excuse will my poor muse devise
If thou dost breathe that pour'st into my verse
Thine own sweet argument, too bad?
  O, give thyself the thanks if aught in me
  Thine own sweet self doth willfully defy.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express my grief.
O, be thou the tenth, eleventh, or twelfth child of my love,
Serving my love with thy sweet appearance only,
Not seeming to me less because I see thee alive;
Therefore I cannot be angry at thee,
Though my very being be assailed; yet be noted
For thy sweet virtue lends not truth to be found,
But truth as a tattered canker lives in love's breath.
  If some suspect of ill masked not thy show,
  Then thou alone kingdoms of hearts shouldst owe.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And in that I do esteem them most.
Thy beauty, being diffused, is most evident
In thy humor and in thy profound truth.
For why should I love someone that doth seem
But in his thoughts, not in his show,
Being dumb, and in his speaking mute?
Or why should I love someone that doth seem
But in his looks, being made from thee,
Fair, and in his substance truth?
  Because thy substance doth in me lie,
  I love that truth which in him doth live.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
And that with myself thou mayst lawfully use
That term which in me doth lie.
For thee, my love, although mine evil be,
Yet love for love doth that which in me doth lie.
Thou that art now my most true love,
Who in thy nature art so far from being
As to qualify for my love's use;
That is, in thy nature dost lie,
Making no record of it to say more.
  But since the log will bear thee witness,
  Make thee another self for me, another true love.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch.
But when her husband's eye is on her,
She runs in pursuit of that same sight,
And hides her beauty even there
Where her husband's cunning eye doth dwell.
Then, if her husband be found,
She is a worthless wife, and he shall have
No love toward her, although she live;
Nay, if she live, she hath lived too short,
And miscalled on him her beauty.
