Those lips that Love's own hand did make
Breathed forth the sound that said "I hate"
To me that languished for her sake;
Utt'ring my true thoughts, making false hearsay,
Casting a ghastly pall on my grave.
Methinks no face so gracious is as hers,
No shape so true, no truth of such account,
That to my misprision she owes everything;
And, so, for her sake, I all alone bequeath,
My life, sold as a monument to love.
  But that is not all; she robs me of that which she loves,
  And, still worse, me of that which she doth esteem.


Poor soul, the center of my sinful earth,
Pressed with these rebel powers that thee array,
Why dost thou pine within and suffer dearth,
When every limb, every part,
Of thy great soul, in thy frame,
Hath his proper respect, in thy brain,
Each part to his part.
O, benefit of ill! Now see what good fortune brings
In poor souls like to thee, that wretched case
Which 'gainst thy self thou stick'st not to change.
  Make thee another self for love of me,
  That beauty still may live in thine or thee.


My love is as a fever, longing still
For that which longer nurseth the disease,
Feeding on that which doth preserve the ill,
And that which brings health to a halt.
I love not only those whom I behold,
Hearing the sweet music played there,
But I in them also read such art
As every tongue doth rehearse,
And master the tongue in every conceit.
  For how can I love those whom I view
  But by their deeds, and by what they say?
  To love is to love what we see,
  And that is this, and this with you remains.


O me, what eyes hath love put in my head,
Which have no correspondence with true sight!
Or if they have, where is my judgment fled,
Which is so grounded in love's love?
O, let me, true in love, answer them:
"Madame, what is 't but love's art
That on inquiry finds truth,
And that truth doubly praised,
By adding one thing to my defect,
Showing my true love to be nothing else but his?"
Madame, sweet love, what fair is it whose shade
Can make love's golden tresses look dun,
Which shames the world and robs princes of treasure?
  Let them ask thee that, then, true in love,
  I love


Canst thou, O cruel, say I love thee not
When I against myself with thee partake?
Do I not think on thee when I forgot thee,
Within the knowledge of mine own heart,
Within the compass of my own love?
Then do I love thee in such sort
As, thou being mine, mine is thy good report,
And for mine own good report do I report
That thou art my all the better part.
That is why, when I find myself come home from jollity,
Thou of such worth gives me such cheer
As, when I perceive myself to be gone,
The world is made more fair by thy worth.
  And so I return again, and do not love thee so
  So oft


O, from what power hast thou this powerful might
With insufficiency my heart to sway?
To make me give the lie to my true sight,
To let myself be fagged of thy love,
And then thyself unscathed keep my love in check.
O, what a torment hath thy heart proved
To be so fitted in thy power,
And what a recompense so extreme
To be unfathered in thy sweet self's pleasure!
Be thou the tenth muse in my love's song,
And I the tenth master enchanter,
Making runes of love's unknown power,
  Forths and runes of love's antique art.


Love is too young to know what conscience is;
Yet who knows not conscience is born of love?
Then, gentle cheater, urge not my amiss,
For all is forgiven where my trespass is.
If you would pardon my crime, dear heart,
Do not accuse me of sin till I have purposed
To do wrong again, though still going on your account.
That is my love. Love is niggard, though built much taller,
As easy to be assailed as to be helped;
It is a babe, not a knowledgable thing,
Which maketh evil thoughts and frowns on good.
  Therefore my behavior towards you is so,
  That you yourself must be my advocate.


In loving thee thou know'st I am forsworn,
But thou art twice forsworn, to me love swearing;
In act thy bed-vow broke, and new faith torn,
As I have sworn in thy womb.
For thee I bring in the cross of two consuls,
Both sworn to thee, but one of them alive,
And he to thee I bring in the canopy of two lovers' eyes,
One of them eyes so fair thou dost desire,
The other so fair but dull,
Which methinks no beauty doth dye.
  How like two widowed maidens doth one dye!
  She that loves him that doth dye, so doth she grow.


Cupid laid by his brand and fell asleep.
A maid of Dian's this advantage found,
And his love-kindling fire did quickly steep
In her bosom and all her dear brood.
Cupid loved her dearly, and slept no more;
But, as the crow flies, he flies in the morning,
And to his robb'ry like a dove doth appear.
  For the sweetest birds in heaven's sweetest air
  Have black feathers, and those which do fly are sweetest.


The little love-god, lying once asleep,
Laid by his side his heart-inflaming brand,
Whilst many nymphs that vowed chaste life to keep
From dancing the eunuch's show,
Had such a look on beauty as his did give.
For though his nakedness seemed to defy all description,
His beauty was so well known
That every tongue says it was by him sung.
And yet, methinks, this wonder of thy sight
Is not enough to make thee happy;
For nimble dancers need not be so swift,
Nor those lilies that fickle daily
To change their state with that sweet weed.
  More must be wanted, more must be desired,
  And so on and so on.


When my love swears that she is made of truth,
I do believe her, though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Who for his love hath no respect to belong.
But when she accuses me of falsehood,
And says I did cheat her husband,
To approve of her husband's art
She proves by my lies that I love her dearer;
And, to prove that I love her less,
Than by her accusing me of loving her,
I'll lie, and say I loved her truth;
  But then, by her accusing me of loving her,
  She proves by my lies that I love her worse.


Two loves I have, of comfort and despair,
That like two spirits do suggest me still.
My better angel is a man right fair,
And like him I love without reserve;
But that same man doth love of me still,
And in that very love doth live another,
A god in love with a mortal wife.
This separation I then do love,
Although my better half in me doth deceive me
And con me of heart and soul,
To the dumb and to the lame seem.
  For how can I then be happy in that which I lack?
  Love is a babe. Then do not be so astonished when I grow old.


