To hear the echoes of my wailing song,
And to think that I too must suffer
When others' voices do witness my wailing?
O, change your mind, my friend; change not the subject.
Shall I not then change my mind, that you may bear with me
While I, heavily pressed, beguile?
Be where I am, friend; I will attend your shows
Even there with my absent thoughts.
  For there lives more joy in one of your graces
  Than both your lovers can in one of their parts give.


