Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
For thee I not only give alms,
But I take pleasure in thy loving thought,
And in that lays my heart in thy furrows,
Where it proudly spends all winter.
Thou hast eyes that I wouldst dearly love had,
But they are thine, and they are fond on my humor,
Which makes me much the more happy.
  All men make faults, and even I in this,
  Thine eyes are more blessed than my graves.


