Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Haply attending on thy sweet self;
For how can mine eyes, which are raven black,
Compare with the golden tresses of thy love's pride?
How can they with thy fair visage graced sit
On thine antique rack, made old for thy sweet date,
Which antique shamefully declines
By the force of thy beauty's growth!
  Yet love knows it is a greater grief
  To bear love's wrong than hate's known injury.


