Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
To the sightless and unrespected place.
Thou gav'st me thine eyes to view,
And, gracious numbers, they view well;
They are thy eyes, and they are thy heart.
Yet love knows it is a vain hope
That these eyes in thy brain can tell
What thoughts write in thy heart;
How many lambs might the stern wolf betray
If like a lamb he could his looks translate!
  But why should I then be grieved at thy eyes,
  Since they were thy true loving kind?


