Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
In witness whereof I am attainted.
For thy sweet, kind, and true heart
As I think hanged on the shame of my crime,
Hath put an idol of beauty before me,
That to thy fair gift I present this written plea,
Proving their love by thy eyes.
  Their love is thine, and they are fools;
  For thee, at thyself so cruel,
  I bring this written plea to show thee.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
To hear my own voice tell the story of my life?
And more, much more, than in my verse can sit
The blot that photographs my life;
Nor can my poor lines to the painted shape
Of my deceased friend's hand, though his picture hung in heaven.
Nor dare I praise the blessed few days that remain
When my friend Ben Jonson, poor as I,
Hath lived in this world and is dead writ.
  But the worth of that is that which it contains,
  And that is this, and this with my friend still lives.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine heir may have
That due to thee which thou dost promise.
Thence comes it that my love, being stol'n from thee,
Comes home again, and by and by clean shorn away
The canopy of myself-love's immured pride.
O, what a happy title do I find,
Happy to have, happy to die!
  But how do I like it when I find
  That my love is so possessed with murd'rous hate,
  That it commits all kinds of theft, bartering away its sweet yield.


