That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And that which they measure by their roseate brows.
But thou, Love, art much clearer in thy judgment.
Thou that art all my love, who art so false
In acknowledging my dearest love as mine,
That I for thee believe'st thou my true love.
This believe'st thou which is not proved;
Then, to be all thine, to be admitted
Thou art much clearer than I in my seeing.
  But what good turns have I had,
  Save that thou hast had me as a swallowed bait.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
