I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Who for his love hath no form of patent,
But is wooed of time, finds no respect
To his friend's style, no surety
But is almost faithless in his words.
I do believe, though in a shadow,
That she loves to look on things as they are,
And that her height, being false,
Is not to her beauty's glory but her cloak
Which hides her parts to deceive.
  She loves to look on things as they are,
  And yet doth such beauty as she shows confound
  Me.


