Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
To that purpose reserved the pleasure of the sight.
How much more praise deserved thy dear heart
If thou couldst answer "This fair child of mine
Shall sum my count, and my lameness alone,"
Proving his love by succession thine.
How much more praise deserved
Than that thou shouldst in my verse bring
To bear that burden which thy love imparts,
Which shall be most thy glory, being dumb.
  For thou art so possessed with my love,
  That all other praise is but mine.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
To be reminded day by day of that which I hate,
When every alien eye
Points on me with his great magnifying glass,
And every tongue says I love thee?
O, let me suffer, poor soul, knowing thy worth,
And then believe me, my love is better than thine.
Look in your glass, and there appears a face
That overgoes my slight ill by much greater;
  And yet, by heaven, I think my ill art greater.


