One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
To take wing again. Lo, thus, having her trimmed,
In her passion she leaps up in the air,
Crawls to her husband's aid, and bravely flies away,
Called back to her place by chance.
--O, what a happy title do I find,
Happy to have the benefit of her beauty,
Happy to have her help, to whom I give a blessing!
  But what's so blessed-fair that fears no blot?
  She's fair, and so is my joy, though she be bad.


