Those lips that Love's own hand did make
Breathed forth the sound that said "I hate"
To me that languished for her sake;
Lips that moved were full of sighs,
But those that fell silent seemed to me
Made dead by Love's speaking.
I was glad, then, when that fell silent,
To hear that same sigh in their voices,
Since every tongue says "This good lady doth die."
But why do they say so, and what can I wish in them
But this my love may make manifest,
  When others but in their wills confound?


Poor soul, the center of my sinful earth,
Pressed with these rebel powers that thee array,
Why dost thou pine within and suffer dearth,
While I, thy sweet self, augmenting store,
Increasing traffic, do hereby halt?
O, do thy worst to steal thyself away,
Fortermilk thyself to the dust, and I am left quite unthrift,
Without thy help, me to my content
Unscathed, nor thou my needy child.
  Pity me, then, and wish I were renewed,
  For fresh blood to give thee life gave.


My love is as a fever, longing still
For that which longer nurseth the disease,
Feeding on that which doth preserve the ill,
Craving that which doth remove the symptom.
That love is as fever a summer's flame,
Resembling as the perfumed tincture of the roses,
But more bold and more persistent,
Since, like a phoenix that peepeth in the west,
It sings hymns at heaven's gate,
Serving with hymns the very same.
  FLOWERY RAY Where pine-trees grow,
  Orchard or field, gay or ugly,
  The yearly journey of FLOWERY RAY parallels.


O me, what eyes hath love put in my head,
Which have no correspondence with true sight!
Or if they have, where is my judgment fled,
That I am some other than in the sight of heaven?
O, me, what rights hath my soul in heaven taken,
That I am not subject to the domination of body,
Or to the slow torture of mortality,
Which doth tie me still more closely to thee?
Be not self-willed, for thou art much too fair
To be assailed by men's eyes;
Give not thyself the glory of being false,
Seeking that beauteous roof to ruinate
Which to repair should be thy chief desire.
  Be not self-willed, for thou


Canst thou, O cruel, say I love thee not
When I against myself with thee partake?
Do I not think on thee when I forgot thee,
The very thought of which makes my crime so cruel?
Lest my love were thoughtless in pursuing
Thy love, which was then the stronger,
Should in the course of my life be smothered
By a thoughtless hate, like a death.
O, that thought might change my life and make
Thy love hate me more, that I might live more happy
Than when I, thee in thy power, do change.
  The thought of that made me wail more,
  To love thee more than hate myself.


O, from what power hast thou this powerful might
With insufficiency my heart to sway?
To make me give the lie to my true sight,
To make me bow before thy shadow,
I summon up every blot on my heart,
And make myself an idol to thy show.
That would be too much, for I love thee so
That I in my love would be ruined,
If like a fool I should give thee that fair show.
O, what a mansion have I got thee,
Where beauty's veil doth cover every blot,
And all things turns to fair that eyes can see!
  Yet could I not so fair a home devise
  As to make myself an idol to thy show.


Love is too young to know what conscience is;
Yet who knows not conscience is born of love?
Then, gentle cheater, urge not my amiss,
Forck in my nerves are th' executor and controlling
Of my deeds to come. Love is not born to hate,
Nor needs death to mend his scythe;
Therefore, love's fall is not from youth to hate,
But is a child of age, strengthened in his sense,
Who, by reason of his own frailties,
Thinks himself virtuous, and so thinks no more.
  Hence, love, be thou child of state.
  Not that I hate you at all,
  But that my love in thee doth lie.


In loving thee thou know'st I am forsworn,
But thou art twice forsworn, to me love swearing;
In act thy bed-vow broke, and new faith torn.
For thee, converted from the thing thou art,
Thou of thyself so base a cheat proved,
Serving nature's own sweet pleasure,
By willful taste her own sweet poison had stol'n.
She bred thee for her seal, and meant thereby
Thou shouldst be thine, not for thyself art;
For thee her jealousy breeds hate,
And she who breeds hate shouldst die.
  Presume not on thy beauty when I see thou bear
  Anger well, though in my heart thou art cruel.


Cupid laid by his brand and fell asleep.
A maid of Dian's this advantage found,
And his love-kindling fire did quickly steep
In cubical wards of her beauty's shame.
Cupid's wounded pride, on seeing this,
Dared to break out in a riot;
But seeing a heavenly guest in wait,
He swerving to and fro like a carrion crow,
Presents his wounded pride to the sight,
And all the rest forgot for fear of ruining his show.
  So they fell a-doting, and a-doting well,
  For fear of ruining their show.


The little love-god, lying once asleep,
Laid by his side his heart-inflaming brand,
Whilst many nymphs that vowed chaste life to keep
From impiety their due,
Were swaying each other's drooping breasts,
Or bending the golden riper's keen eye
To gaze on his new-appearing tomb.
The lily, on which thy breast doth lie,
Hath thy sweet face almost touched;
But, for some strange reason,
The sweetest tender niggard fears his love's return.
  For fear of which, hear this, thou age unbred:
  Thou shalt be sweet in thy breast age, and still be loved of many.


When my love swears that she is made of truth,
I do believe her, though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Speaking of which I am assured will be false,
If I do but perceive her truth in moving.
In so far as I consent to believe
The lameness of her false argument,
Though I know it to be false,
The cause of my believing is this:
  She knows I am a true heart;
  And having her, I would be a fool not to believe.


Two loves I have, of comfort and despair,
That like two spirits do suggest me still.
My better angel is a man right fair,
And like him I love still; but he is no longer mine,
Since my constant presence persuaded him so.
He lends me virtue, and that is all;
But he loves but me alone,
And that is all. Then, if for my sake he depart,
I love him not more, though he depart;
I give him leave to depart,
And him leave I think but ill.
  For him my love is not enough,
  To have that I must mutual give.


