Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
To thee my all-too-appreciated sorrow.
Thine eyes, that taught the dumb on high to sing
And heavy ignorance aloft to fly,
Have added feathers to the learned's wing
And given grace a double majesty.
How much more doth beauty need from thee,
The storehouse wherewith it must be builded
To perfection within the flattery of thy mind.
  You are the fairest and most precious jewel
  That eyes can impart, and they that have seen
  You have all the knowledge that eyes can find.


