For thee, my sweet love, my presentiment is
That thou in some shady closure to time will call,
And I in some secret hope tell it to thee
Thy true object is to frame another self
Whose beauty and truth shall in time be made
By the time when my sweet love's fresh case
Grows cold and my despairing hand droves away.
  But hope, in such case, grows delusional;
  And even from his delusional hope
  The rainy morrow that sourly confronts me.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Even to wet a tender spot,
Lest thy golden tresses should again be rent,
And needy threads should once more be lost,
