Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
To drown my sorrow with their sad cheer.
But, ah, thou art fairer than they,
And to thy fair I assign the more;
Therefore to thy sweet I aspire;
To 'cide this title I cry amen,
Not from love, but from kindliness to thee.
Thou art my all the world, and I must strive
To know my shames and praises from thy tongue;
  For, like candles burned in summer's night,
  Mine eyes are windows to my heart's eye.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
With my love dead and my friend still living?
No, it is necessary that my friend should suffer
From mine own ill deeds, to make me bear
These worse than self-love; so he bears
Mine own ill deeds, and I am forced
To do things myself to bear them alone.
Then may I not boast that I am happier
Than those whom nature hath made for to sorrow,
Beggared of pleasing thoughts to bless;
  Being thankful to thy doings,
  I am blamed if I forget to thank thee.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May be free from all blame, too sinful to be remembered.
How much more praise deserved my love and homage
If I could but kiss his hand, true love recognizing
His form in some windows of thine mind!
O, then give me leave to sigh, true; but
For my sake to him I'll vow one thing,
And that is, "I will be true to him."
  So true a love as this mine can deceive
  That he may look into my thoughts and say "This artist lies;
  Painting false, like his mistress, false the heart."


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
For to my love nothing is wanting,
And to thyself thy own worth a ransom.
Thy love is all mine, and nothing else's,
And so I engraft it into a new birth.
So then my love engrafted into this new birth
A new covering to his fresh appearance;
But what he truly is, he doth live,
And bears his own fresh incident to show.
  Thus do I engraft love into another's womb,
  And that another's love into mine.


