And when they learn to question thee
Find thy spirit, which nebulous change doth afflict,
With shifting shadows upon him appear.
  Then thou know'st thy estimate;
  The deepest trench trenches have thorns that fester.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
But not what true beauty is;
Therefore their eyes are but betrayers,
Of a thing not fully understood.
Then, when thy love is done,
The rest is but play of the mind;
But when the mind is done playing,
The rest is nothing but smoke and nonsense.
  For, as the painter paints,
  the subject is but his form,
  And, to the general audience, his show lies.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Or some blind beastly love, besmeared with sluttish time,
As with a monarch's gold worth;
But still I love her as if she were butter
Cooked with ethereal spices,
Making some sort of sweet patriotic hush
With sentimental eyes confounded;
  And nothing hinders my love but my love's strength.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Incite not hatred in me, though my name be wrong,
But forgive me all insults where I am blamed,
Though'r my tongue tell the contrary.
For thou art my all the world, and my love is all the world's wealth,
My speaking all world's knowledge,
My heart the key to all knowledge,
The conquest of all hearts.
  For there can live no hatred in thine or mine
  So long as love lives and is warmed in my breast.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy bitter thoughts more lustily than thoughts of love.
If I do fall a-doting on thy praise,
Upon thy part with thanksgiving vow shall stay,
Though love's true color, like winter's green,
Beyond all date and all understanding.
Look what thy thoughts have writ in flower
Within thine own bud; yet be mindful
Of others' verses, which have more Poise
Than those of thy sweet lips.
  To most faces this seems strange,
  Not to say more than looks show.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
