Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And, sure as fickle stars, keep their pace.
Yet, like stones of worth, they thinly placed are,
Or otherwise placed hold not a treasure;
So thou, sure of nothing, hast put in place
A palace for souls to wait in,
Which shall be most their treasure, being least in thy control.
  Then, if for their treasure thou partaker,
  They shall find thee out some day,
  Though thou fool this time still more.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Unarmed against the war of truth and her own worth.
But when she tells me I am made of one thing only,
And shows me no strip but of one colour,
I become aflame with indignation,
And swear I never saw she painted such a face.
She might as well have removed my hair as paint,
Hiding my blood and making me seem dead.
  To thee I bear all ill graces gladly attached,
  Even to thy well-known counterfeit.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Sland'ring me with thy lies, as thou didst throw
In my bosom's rear,
Is more civil than injury.
For, love, we are one; yet, like members of one family,
We do not grudge each other's society,
Nor do we keep score of wrongs done;
So your unfair abuse of my heart
May still be better than your false praise.
  For not to be praised introduces loss;
  And, lose, thou lose the only praise I need.


