Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
For wanting what I most have,
Thyself thy friend, to whom I am constrained
To shake hands with time's expired revolt;
More, yet more, than enough are those bonds,
Which for their own sake do tie me up here,
Some where else I would be free.
Thyself, to whom I am confined,
Works in my health, gives blood, and keeps me awake.
  But thee, that loathsome parasite,
  Kill me with spites, yet we must not be foes.


