I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Without all assistance from mentor glass,
Stirred by bold youth into this suicidal rhyme;
So should my verse, wrought in haste,
Be scorned, like a sad widow whose husband doth pass,
And wearied with his toil,
As if from his shame not to be found.
O, if I could write the beauty of thy mind,
And in fresh numbers number all thy wondrous leaves,
Reserve them for my love, not for their rhyme,
  For fear of which, having them, I would not sell.


