Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
Of his shady embassy to thee,
The fostered son of her own love;
As he goes, she keeps him near her heart,
Nor flies him far, for he doth teach her constancy.
Then, if for his sake she will not forsake him,
She keeps him still, and his beauty yokes her to this course:
She ties up her spring, and her lemon blushing shows;
Then, when he is unclothed with scythe-tattered age,
She lets him decay in ills of woe.


