Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Attending on thee with jealous looks;
Yet in good faith my sorrow finds room
And walks with sweet grace, seeming to groan;
So that my friend sorrow may seem to me
To have gone aground on an unruly woe,
The like of which never took place before.
Thine eyes, when thou, my friend, wilt leave,
Do not forsake me then, for thou art my all the world;
I cannot then depart without thee,
Lest my bewailed sorrow should do thee shame,
  And thou, too, with my loss be comforted


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
For I am so strongly in love that I cannot tell
Whether my friend hath escaped or not.
Then should I faint, and care not who finds out
The way to my hymn, though my verse be unused;
For there can live no hatred in thine eye;
Therefore in that I cannot weep nor groan aloud
When thou, my friend, hath struck me so.
  But if my friend be dead, my verse shall be
  But song of awkwardly woe, with too short a bow.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
