And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine might take.
So then I am at a loss how to prove him,
And, to be sure that my word shall not be disbelieved,
I must by deeds prove him here above a swore.
So then I leave you, poor but free,
And take my love and my liberty with me;
So love for love, yea, give me that honor even where I am.
  But be contented when other proves me false,
  Because my love is such that I cannot undo it.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
For whatsoever is not thy will,
I do with mine own feeling keep.
Thy glass will show thee the stain
Which thy shadow will truly be;
Your features with me will bear that record,
And I in thy visual park will be forgot.
Never believe, though in my nature reigned
All frailties that besiege all kinds of blood,
That it could so preposterously be stained
To leave for nothing all thy sum of good.
  For nothing this wide universe I call,
  Save thou, my rose; in it thou art my all.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
