Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Tan me with their false colour.
Thine eyes, that for thy sake taught art
To dumb and miserable meadows my days;
Those same eyes that for thy sake tricked me
Instealing thy beauty and thy wealth from me;
They have hoisted my transport bridge
To high planes of merit, where thou art not.
I love thee in such sort
As, thou being mine, mine is thy good report;
I thank thee for that which I hope thou wilt prove.
  But be thou wary, for thou art advised
  Not to be too much touched with my praise.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
Without accusing thee of injury?
Just let me suffer in silence alone,
And thou, in silence, forgive my woe.
Speak of thy lameness and I straight will halt,
Against thy reasons making no defense.
Thou canst not, love, disgrace me half so ill,
To set a form upon desired change;
I am quite reconciled to thine understanding,
And so should thy peace and my pardon prove.
  I am tame, though thou art powerful,
  To be drubbed and treated like a beast of burden.


