So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine own self
May under thee control arise.
The cause of this strange separation is my love or desire,
Which is so strong that it almost touches self-love;
Self so touched it self-subjugate is,
Sap checked with sullied cup.
O, that our day of love might be remembered
When other selves had passed away!
  But that our love alone should so seem
  To take away that which is most dear.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
To have thy love, though thou none lov'st me then.
Thou canst not vex me with inconstant mind,
Since that my life on thy revolt doth lie.
O, what a happy title do I find,
Happy to have thy love, happy to die!
But what's so blessed-fair that fears no blot?
Be as thy presence is, gracious and kind,
Or else as thyself self-loving prove!
  To be obsequious in this case is to be sick.


