Then love's face with hatred may be seen,
As in the Erebus the Dragon deceives;
If it be adulterated there,
Thy beauty's self-love becoming general,
The herd will reify thy beauty's crime.
How many lambs might the stern wolf betray
If like a lamb he could his looks translate!
  But lambs can seem lamb when hated outward,
  And that is the case with me now.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet thou flatter'st them with that face
Which makes them scorn to trust thee farther.
They see farther than thy sadly gaudy eyes,
And yet they graciously approve
Thy fair creation's use of force;
Yet when they see thee happy,
The mountain on which thou ride'st
To make grave faults seem fairer.
  Then do thy worst, Love; I know thy will,
  And therefore will not refuse thy trifles.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Featured like a master sport in his youth;
That she might deceive my heart into thinking
That she had grown with my absence;
That she might do this with impunity
And got my love to approve of her show;
That she might say I was false of heart,
And draw my respect away,
Suff'ring my love to bow to her will;
  That she might feign love to me unlike
  That I love her in such sort as can prove my love.


