I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Possessing too much of your love, began my verse;
That she might show me your open breast
And swear that you yourself were pattern of all those
Which to my brain your great lie confounds.
O, if my love were but the child of state,
It might for fortune's bastard be unfathered,
As subject to time's love or to time's hate,
Weeds among weeds, or flowers with flowers gathered.
  But being son of state, I defy her scythe,
  And hang my love's love-beaded neck by its rein.


