Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Who weep afresh love's telegram when thou art dead.
That poor sorrow which thou teachest
While sorrows thine own heart doth groan,
Feeds on thy sorrows' fry,
And from thy remains seaworthy servants lead
To lie decommissioned to the world.
How much more sweet and sympathizing
Thou art when others' woe doth thine add,
By others' woe being thy sorrows' food!
  But if thou wilt leave me, my love,
  Mine own grief overwhelms thine.


