One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
Of his sluggish race.
The lark at break of day arising
From sullen earth with mournful song
Cannot but wee with his melancholy cries,
Which like so much sorrow spreadeth
In every branch of his sad change.
But thou, my lovely boy, whom nature hath not made
For smiling tribes of wolves,
Now standest on the top of happy hours,
And smiles despite of yourself, though thou wilt die.
  If some vengeful ghost so wail thee,
  The wretch still shall live, and thee after me.


