Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
They attend on thee with sad looks,
Without all seeming care or pity.
I perceive their sad thoughts, and they have discerned
Thy true will, which is to me exactly right;
They look on thee with sad eyes,
And with sad countenance inform me
That thou, truly miserable, wilt end miserably.
  But, ah, thought I not mad,
  To give away myself for nothing?


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
For then my friend hath no part, though I die?
O, no, it is enough that he shall hear my moan,
And then my verse, which now sings sweet love to thee,
Shall give another place. Then thou too, my love, give another place;
Thy sweet lip and tongue again shall live,
As before, and thou in another's rhyme
