Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Those whose looks with thee I loved I seen away.
In black night my love shone brightly,
But, for my sake, in day it dimmed;
In black night my love shone more bright
Than when, in thee, my eye could not see.
In day my love was lusty, but in night it was rude,
Made brawling by thy open eyelids.
In day my love was gracious, but in night
Cannot remember aught of thee.
  But day my love remembered;
  And night thy love did shadow things prove.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
When others can do nothing but moan and wink?
Nay, if they look sad, they look wretched company,
For I must every day look on them with pity,
They have no caring care for my welfare;
They have no friends, either inward or outward,
That they may spend the weekend wailing my fate;
Nor can they complain that I do not give them leave
To chat with myself over their sorrow.
  But yet, know, sweet love, I do grant they feel it;
  They are ill-treated in their hearts.


