Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
With my pain still fresh, even in the midst of sweetness?
Let me suffer, like a condemned wretch,
And yet not be condemned to die,
For that tongue that tells the story of my life
To the very same person that once told it to me,
That tells it to my friend, too well;
Then not be blamed when I am judged worthy
Of that which I prognosticate.
  The worth of that is that which it contains.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine remain.
For thou art so possessed with murd'rous hate
That 'gainst myself I can do nothing,
Nor dare to mock that hatred which bears me above.
O, that our night of woe might have remembered
My deepest sense how hard true sorrow hits,
And soon to me as I to thee then tendered
The humble salve which wounded bosoms fits!
  But that your trespass now becomes a fee;
  Mine ransoms yours, and yours must ransom me.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Finding faults where I vouchsafe thee mine,
And there lies my glory, more than bare truth;
For to thy pure love I bring in sense--
Thy adverse party is thy advocate--
And 'gainst myself a lawful plea commence.
Then, beauteous defendant, how I forgive thee
If ever thou shalt pollute me with suspect--
Or, if thou not pollute me, at least do thy worst
To impair my pleasure, which is thy sole motive.
  I do forgive thy robb'ry, gentle thief,
  Although thou steal thee all my poverty


