Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet they in their wills withhold it from thee.
For would they not so, for fear of thy sight,
For thou to thyself art so possessed
With murd'rous hate, arising from thy seeing?
They would be deceived, love, were it not thy wiliness
To set a form upon necessary change,
Making shadows lovely things purer than they are?
  Since they well know what true love is,
  Thou swart tyrant over them, and they must bear it down.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
She might question my reason in a doubting mood,
Ask me why I do what I do,
And I would reply that I do it because I love you so.
Then she might say well I do love you so,
And I would beguile her with a thousand lies,
To make her love me more than she knows.
Love is a fools' gambit,
Whilst folly, born of love, thinks it safe.
  I would beguile her with a thousand lies,
  Then think that I do love you too much to lie.


