I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Without scholarship or learning to teach;
That she might suppose my speaking words to be
Commit to a confined rage,
And derive all my compliments from memory;
That she might suspend my disbelief
And draw all my praises to myself alone.
But since she keeps me in suspense,
My surmise is confirmed:
She is made of truth. I cannot doubt
But that in her breast lies the quality
Which separates me from false painting.
  And therefore my love's color me alters,
