Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
And I in thee abide with myself alone!
Then, if for my sin thou my love comply,
I in thee understand not the hate,
Nor do I rightly term thy worth with mine own.
For if thou get a husbandry in me,
A true husband is not love toward thee,
But happy I, that love and am beloved
Within thine own sweet sexual feeling.
  But if thou depart, mine absence is welcome,
  And thine abundant provide will be graced be.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
Of her dependant flowers.
But thou, set on thine own bright side,
How would thy beauty's rose show
To the clear day with thy much clearer light
When to unseeing eyes thy shade shines so!
How would, I say, mine eye be blessed made
By looking on thee in the living day,
When in dead night thy fair imperfect shade
Through heavy sleep on sightless eyes doth stay!
  All days are nights to see till I see thee,
  And nights bright days when dreams do show thee me.


