Those lips that Love's own hand did make
Breathed forth the sound that said "I hate"
To me that languished for her sake;
Then how I bruised when I saw thee frown,
Whenas thy sweet face seemed to me
But pattern of some serious preface!
O, take heed, dear heart, of this verse,
For it goes sometimes too far and leaves out part
Of thy sweet humor. When I consider how much more
Than public speaking tongue could sum up
In single words thy lovely sense,
The cause of my pain is revealed:
  My heart is sick of that which it doth hold.


Poor soul, the center of my sinful earth,
Pressed with these rebel powers that thee array,
Why dost thou pine within and suffer dearth,
Since going on in thy course thou art sure to wane?
Seeking rest beneath this burning sun
Which machines thy beauty to march in line,
I fires into thee my all-contented fire,
And thou with me, my all-contented wing;
We both soar; he in the main of his might
Points on thee with shady aspect,
And thou in me, my wish-ful thinking breast,
With smiling missionaries courses onwards to liberty.
  But whether from longitude or latitude thou fly'st,
  The heat is not so extreme that thou nor I die.


My love is as a fever, longing still
For that which longer nurseth the disease,
Feeding on that which doth preserve the ill,
As sailor frugal on the niggard ship's fuel,
Or as getter of big gainer's treasure,
Who in his thoughts dwelleth
But what he receives in bits and pieces,
Like a eater of rotten.
I have seen roses damasked, red and white,
But no such roses see I in thee;
And in some perfumes is there more delight
Than in the breath that from my love reeks.
  Since love is such a babe that sometimes it dies,
  Let it live, and then treasure it treasure its treasure.


O me, what eyes hath love put in my head,
Which have no correspondence with true sight!
Or if they have, where is my judgment fled,
That I may correct their gross imperfection?
O, none, unless this miracle have might,
That in my thought there runs a twain
Which truth in outward form doth refute!
In my soul's thought there lives a joy
Which truth doth not proudly show,
Nor gives to outward form the due;
O, no, it is buried deep,
Under the cross, where it cannot be seen.
  For it lives alone, and is blessed
  Where it is not worshiped with looks.


Canst thou, O cruel, say I love thee not
When I against myself with thee partake?
Do I not think on thee when I forgot to say so,
When I was famished for a look,
And having had it, felt it no longer?
If the thought of thee told me I loved thee not,
I would be a changed person;
For willing minds at pleasure do change their state,
And that right which persists
Though alteration be madness.
In so profound abysm I throw all care
Of others' voices that my adder's sense
To thee and to myself alone sayeth.
  Love is not love that touches another's face,
  But it is that which makes others' voices grow.


O, from what power hast thou this powerful might
With insufficiency my heart to sway?
To make me give the lie to my true sight,
To make me acknowledge my sinful state,
Which thou hast so grossly disregarded.
O, what fearful disgrace wouldst thou prove
Were it not thy eternal love that gave scope
To offend the most sensitive breast,
The very temple of my heart,
Which but today by thy being absent
Makes willing warriors out of my love to hate!
  But what's so sacred about thee
  That thou neglects to take that due
  From me that thou o'ergreen'st.


Love is too young to know what conscience is;
Yet who knows not conscience is born of love?
Then, gentle cheater, urge not my amiss,
Since that you yourself, being made from love, feel'st
Like heirs to some virgin womb.
O, give my love another name,
And better prove thy worth than you yourself give it;
For I may never more know thy mind,
Lest I, too much profane, should do it wrong
And haply of our old acquaintance tell.
  For thee, against myself I'll vow debate,
  For I must ne'er love him whom thou dost hate.


In loving thee thou know'st I am forsworn,
But thou art twice forsworn, to me love swearing;
In act thy bed-vow broke, and new faith torn,
Yet heretic love's not so evident.
For in me two goddesses do stand,
One for love, the other for crime;
And in our love's rebellious strong hand
Doth sit the ally of my betrayer.
In us two queenly loves do live,
One for love, the other for crime;
And in our love's boldness' veins do lie,
One for love, the other for crime.
  Incapable of more, replete with thee,
  My most true mind thus maketh mine eye untrue.


Cupid laid by his brand and fell asleep.
A maid of Dian's this advantage found,
And his love-kindling fire did quickly steep
In vengeful ignorance.
The thorn thus with care of honor grew
In fide's defaced beauty's field,
And all things turns to fair that eyes can see.
But now her sweet action brings to mind
A still more heinous crime,
And all things turns to fair that eyes can see.
  She looks on him thus, and he to her shines bright
  As the sun in the west, with sovereign power over heads.


The little love-god, lying once asleep,
Laid by his side his heart-inflaming brand,
Whilst many nymphs that vowed chaste life to keep,
Without his help, their ceremony did defy;
And, terror-struck, they buried him deep,
Within the deep end of love's spear,
That thereby the rude warrior might not boast.
But thou, to whom my love is,
Most worthy comfort, now my greatest grief,
Thou best of churls best of loving guys
Art able to provoke my grief more;
For blowing in thyress' dregs' wake
Is like a pumpkin in winter's near,
Or a soothsayer in early morn.
  But if thy grief be true love's


When my love swears that she is made of truth,
I do believe her, though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Made of worse sort of clay,
She might ornament her beauty with rose;
But what's most true in love is,
And that is this, and this with thee remains,
The single irreconcilable thing.
If she promise to be true,
A thing so heinous that I cannot believe,
She keeps her word, though I swear she loves me nothing.
  But if she be false, and on me proved,
  I never writ, nor no man ever loved.


Two loves I have, of comfort and despair,
That like two spirits do suggest me still.
My better angel is a man right fair,
Who in smiling countenance delights in smiling sight,
And in other accents fits in familiar tune.
Another loves, more bright than her mate,
Whose virtue black spoteth not, though in himself most bright.
A third, although he with her singleness doth stay,
On her sparkling hindquarters doth float a notion
That beauty may blush wherever she abide.
A spirit so possessed is he who heavily doth ride,
And looks on goodness with beauteous countenance,
Which seeming injury unblesseth him and him alone.
