Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
With accents of sorrow and bitterness;
For thy sweet face with black shall have
And thy youth blushing shame, when thou art old
And look'st other peoples' faces with suspicion.
For thyself, to whom thy beauty hath art assigned,
My love is as my ransom, and my love's sufferance
As my exodus, when I return in that state
Which art now my joy and thy love's hope.
  But if that hope be impeded,
  The hardest knife ill used doth lose his edge.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
For that I am still with you, and you are with me still,
Still talking of that which gives offense;
So shall I, still with you, bear the cross,
And yet be paid for that which I compile.
No love toward others in that I witness calls
My sorrows, though that friend hath so placed
In my pleasing thought, that my sling becomes
A muse to mouthed sorrows and endless hours.
  Then gladly would I be the lark,
  To sing hymns at heaven's gate when other birds sang.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May not do so terribly wrong.
For him thy love is not a grief,
Nor can it bring to my root what is most keen,
That it doth nothing to give to the see,
O'er incertainty and rent by time.
He is too dear for that, too dear for this,
And so are you, to him at least sufficiently dear.
He is to me as father is to child,
Or as sweet-seasoned showers are to the ground;
And for myself I'll live as a tomb
Or as a ruined grave is to the soul.
 


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
For what I want, thou hath thy desire,
And for that love of thine I cling.
And for that love of thine I hold my tongue,
Since, perjured or not, I cannot be false
And hang my praises where thou art.
O, that our night of woe might have remembered
My deepest sense how hard true sorrow hits,
And soon to thee as thou art to me tendered
The humble salve which wounded bosoms fits!
  But that thy love, which stands so strong,
  Makes frailer horses forget their fugitives.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
There can live no hatred in thine eye;
Therefore in that I cannot know thy change.
In many's looks, the false soul's history
Is writ in moods and frowns and wrinkles strange.
But heaven in thy creation did decree
That in thy face sweet love should ever dwell;
Whate'er thy thoughts or thy heart's workings be,
Thy looks should nothing thence but sweetness tell.
  How like Eve's apple doth thy beauty grow,
  If thy sweet virtue answer not thy show.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And that it doth profit them both;
But thou, love, know'st not how to make one twain
By praising his beauty, desiring his love's scope,
Or how to make one twain excel another,
By praising his youth, hoping his skill will blossom
In this, his glory.
Thou canst not, love, make one youth
Or one beauty equal,
For they are one, and so shall thou be
If ever two, ever two, live in thine.
  Make thee one, then, dear heart, but do not so;



When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Applying truths to things not seen,
Making clothes of mendacity,
Gilding pale cheeks with complexion dead
And making women's eyes shadowed,
Hiding thy golden tresses in lusty night,
Making lily lips so white as wish they were headed
And adding lust to lily's budding hue.
But when my love says she is made of lies,
I consider it no more a doubt
Then love's perjured gift, stolen from thee,
Hath sweet authority, being vouched for thee.
  In her love's strength I swear I do adore thee,



O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
I will not praise thy lovely argument
Unless I have thy love in my thought,
And therein be perjured.
Be thou as thy presence is, gracious and kind,
Or to thyself at least kind-hearted prove,
Making exemptions where thou mayst prove
And profess that thou didst ever live in sin.
Yet do thy worst, love; yet do thy worst
Not to show thy face to the world,
  Unless this general evil thou doest
  While to thyself thou stick'st the fault.


