Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
As miners whose eye or heart doth corrupt.
And, for their self-love esteeming
Thou, themselves, art all the better part of me;
For thou art all the better part of me
Whilst my poor, mediocre rhyme, lacking all praise,
Makes worms thine apple, sweet and ugly.
  But if worms eat thee, they cannot kill thee;
  But if they sense thee not, they lose all sense.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
After my death, to render me tongue-tied,
Sap checked with scars, like a sick man?
No, if that were my thinking, it were folly,
To set a form on desirable change,
That being debarred the benefit of rest,
I should in my decay live alone,
Without help of any kind.
O, let me suffer, being lame,
Without any pity from your heart,
And believe me, I love you more than death itself.
  Therefore, like a mortal and truly sorry,
  I will weep to have that which I most love


