Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
Of needy lambs and helpless wolves;
So I, to whom thou hast given so good a home,
Take all my comfort of thy worth and truth.
Yet be not so proud of that which I compile,
Nor make thy worth a measure by my rhyme or satire.
Thy worth, though great, is not my argument;
It is thy truth that gives sweet pleasure
To those that hear it and to those that read it.
  For thee, and for mine own well-being,
  This written embassage is of no more worth than honey.


