And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May suspect no fear from thy breast;
Thy body is what I lease, and that is
Where I live, and that belongs to thee.
For where is she so fair whose face
Not thy memory yet are stol'n from thy mind?
Or who is he so fond will be thy heir
If thou date no more to be remembered.
Thou art thy body, and that is where thou art held,
Or else other men's eyes should mar the painted banquet.
  And yet methinks thou art more lovely and more blood than eyes can tell.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
For wanting thy will, though thou art removed;
And having thee, of my hate no joy then mine.
Thou canst not vex me with inconstant mind,
Since that my life on thy part is Ended.
I have a spirit of love, and therefore wish you well;
Yet hate that I hope you never will be,
Nor evermore acknowledge me.
  Hence my love, to whom thou art not happy,
  Nor thy sweet self to me.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Then may I dare to boast how I do love thee;
Thy beauty, thy worth, being crowned with thee,
Is assured mine and mine alone.
For if thy worth, being crowned with me,
Are gates of steel so strong that we cannot cross,
Thence shall I know my kind of love is weak,
And thence this rivalry shall stay.
  For sweet love knows not distance,
  And thus we must hinder our fleshly woe.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And how it lives, which thou dost hide.
Thou blindfold thyself, and there appears a face
That shapely shine on thy roughness;
Then, beauteous niggard, why dost thou abuse
The bounteous largess given thee to give?
Thou that art now the world's jewel turneth
To make my love's jade more green.
  And so, almost incessantly, thou grow'st
  To sparkling stones, which thou crowd'st to thine eye.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Stirred by a painted beauty to his verse,
Who with her soft argument afloat doth stand
Against the strong hand of time,
Stealing away the treasure of her husbandry.
O, let her think that I am some juvenile verse
Who sing in the ranks of grown men,
And even so her love is inflated,
That it seems to me as if I were new made.
  Let her believe, though I know she lies,
  That I may be false in that I love her.


