For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And that's all the better part of me.
If I have ranged, every trifle younger,
In quest of some object not my purpose,
Th' expense of spirit in a thing so petty,
Robbing myself of half my content,
Makes me more solitary, more a fool,
Saparaniing my wealth with ornamentary deeds.
I never saw a greater waste of spirit
Than in thy chief, where everything is added new.
  And yet I think mine eye well enough to love
  Though it doth deceive me.


