O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Wherein thou canst true love show,
Attendent on thy own sweet virtue.
Then, when my thoughts are troubled
With thoughts of mine own love breaking loose,
Thy sweet virtue, though it alone liveth,
Shall things seem better to me than hate,
Now that I think on thy dear merit.
Thou art my all the world, and I must strive
To know my shames and praises from thy tongue;
  Or else be a hypocrite, happy me alone.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
Of that sickly babe with her sickly knife,
Prepared to guard that which was guarded.
But when that tender Lamb of his begins to wane,
The cruel knife that murdered his lover doth give another place.
That tender Lamb that loved and was beloved
No longer hopes to be fed by that same knife,
But cries amen to be hacked and torn asunder,
Repeat th' same at a later date.
  And that tender Lamb will continue to cry amen
  If ever that knife with my love shall find a new lease.


