And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Forbase against myself I now leap;
No sooner do I dock my rud'st,
Than that which I most enjoy contented least.
Then, were I not dumb, suffering under thy cross,
Without thinking on thy pleasure, I would play the fool,
And in my verse praise thy worth.
But now, spite of myself, I think on thee,
And make mine eye thine eyes even so.
  Then thou breakers' delight, or my love's woe,
  Which in thy choice are on thee simultaneously.


