Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And that it is compounded of many elements.
They see the starry heaven above,
And the marvellous earth whereon it orbits,
Resembling tall towers set in the air,
Which Antichrist's fleet could not admiringly climb;
They see the hungry ocean which encloses the kingdom
Of Poseidon, where huge fish like to thee stand,
And fill the hollow leaves of every bough with thy hair.
  Yet they neither see nor know
  The heart that animates them all.
  In them all is our love, and therein they have all knowledge.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Without knowing my name or where I am.
So I leave her, and travel forth
Without all parental control,
To find out whether love is made of words or fabric.
I find it is the former, though more fickle,
Which loves to praise itself most;
I find it to be the most basic feature
Of all love's workings, whether it be wheat or swine.
  And so I return and find my love gone,
  Which, being gone, no one can blame me for.


