Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Since thou dost pity me and I am hated
Not for thy sake, but for thy cause.
Thou dost pity me, then, not for my sake,
But for my sake, and for my sake choose
Thyself to be my advocate;
Then can I vexation quit me,
And patient ills yield me graces.
  But if I have any grief at all,
  The worst was this: my grief was my love's cause.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
For with my friend imprisoned from thee,
I must attend time's leisure with my moan,
Wishing me like to one more rich in hope,
Featured like him, like him with friends possessed,
Desiring this man's art and that man's scope,
With what I most enjoy contented least;
Yet in these thoughts myself almost despising,
Haply I think on thee, and then my moan
Comes faster than at first, more strong, far greater.
  Then thou who art now my heart's guest
  Unlooked on diest unless thou take that away.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May privilege thy services with regard to me,
And other men's interest with regard to thee.
For how do I hold myself harmless
Whilst that this shadow doth such substance give
That I in thy freedom am sufficed
And by a part of all thy glory live.
The cause of this happy result
Is this, that I am your slave and suspect no
But that thou, thyself controlling,
Art what I depend on to do.
  This thought is as a death, which cannot choose
  But weep to have that which it fears to lose.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
More than thy love can make me proud,
More than thy envy can make me tongue-tied,
More than thy neglect can make me shiver;
And having thee, I must strive
To know my shames and praises from thy tongue;
No praise whatsoever shall I find in that tongue
That can make me any sourest or most unfathered.
Then, if for my pleasure thou my stay abide,
The worst was this to me:
  That I might see what poor services did cunning Monsieur render
  In connivance with his sweet quality.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Give not a windy night a rainy morrow,
To linger out a purposed overthrow.
If thou survive my well-contented day,
When that churl Death my bones with dust shall cover,
And shalt by fortune once more resurvey
These poor rude lines of thy deceased lover,
Compare them with the bett'ring of the time,
And though they be outstripped by every pen,
Reserve them for my love, not for their rhyme,
  Since I would not dull thee with their praise.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And whether it be fadeless or lasting.
They know how to please the eye they view,
And, churls, their hearts are thine.
Another face they view, another tongue,
And, constant stars, in each they judge;
And, most true churls, in all they know
True things proceed from true minds.
  Therefore, love, be thou thine;
  Even so, churls; but do not so.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Without fortune or men's eyes,
Without clerical fear, or anything else;
That she might think me some worthless song,
Without all praise, though she did bless
The small rituals that used to bless her.
But since she swears such a one thing,
That she might think me some thing other than she is,
I must acknowledge her, though I do not bless her with due.
  But so thou, my verse, mayst write
  That she may praise thee despite thy lies.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Make me tongue-tied speaking of thy blessings and praises,
Excusing thy transgressions more than thy sins are.
O, know, sweet love, I love thee in such sort
As, thou being mine, mine is thy good report;
So, me being thy witness, write not that I am mad,
Excusing thy sins more than thy sins are.
  For to thy sensual fault I bring in sense--
  Thine adverse party is thy advocate--
  And 'gainst myself a lawful plea commence.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Too much; therefore be sick of mind,
And do not receiv any more of thy truth.
If I have any grief at all to report,
It is not from thy bitterness that I feel,
Nor do I blame those who praise thee but me,
Since I alone know thy worth,
And therefore maintain'st myself most rich in knowledge.
  But if I be blamed for my tongue,
  The fault is mine, and mine alone shall bear the weight of thy praise.


