That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And make it their fashion to go about
In search of that which they see not.
O, love, whence didst thou steal thy sense of sight,
If not from thyself thy sweet self-love?
Return, forgetful muse, and straight redeem
In gentle numbers time so idly spent;
Sing to the ear that doth thy lays esteem
And gives thy pen both skill and argument.
  Give thyself a namesake blessing,
  Since whate'er thy glass shows, it is thee.


