One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
To tend her husband's breed,
Which fastidious slave will not taste
At first the very least dirty.
Then, when his shocked eyes have gazed on thee
And are famished for a look,
The rich soil where they took root
Must yield him some instant nitrogen,
Which, more than appetite, gives him life and light.
Then, were not nature's bank a sewing closet,
With wretched lock she opens her bosom,
And, sullied with their odor,
Sets down her proud embroidered cheek,
Which often shows its age by reason of decay.
 


