For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
With my lay-about friend for comfort,
Who well may be my foe when I am wounded?
O, let me suffer, supposing thou art true,
Thyself to be my sufferer,
And then be a benefactor to me,
Knowing thy heart too well to bear love's wrong.
Be where thou art not, though I know thou art,
And I will be a flatterer too;
  Receiving thee as a dream doth flatter,
  In sleep a king, but waking no such matter.


