One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
Of that which fastidious care should keep secret.
But when that tiredness with which she brings forth
Her precious children, her young lover's eyes
Are everything edible to that which she doth catch.
And, for their nourishment, she dumps them in the world,
Feeds on the arrack of youth,
And lives upon the proceeds of idle age.
  So true a fool is love that in his lies,
  When he takes delight in thy sportive days.


