Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Sap checked with frost, and lusty leaves quite gone,
Beauty o'er-snowed and bareness everywhere.
And for the peace of thy souls I hold such strife
As 'twixt a miser and his wealth is found:
Now proud as an enjoyer, and anon
Doubting the filching age will steal his treasure;
Now counting best to be with thee alone,
Then bettered that the world may see my pleasure.
  For thee, against myself I send this:
  My joy is thy outward part,
  And my joy's right,


