Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Thy beauty and thy years full well befits,
For still temptation follows where thou art.
The beast that bears me, tired with my love's picture,
Plods dully on, to bear that weight in me,
As if by some instinct the wretch did know
His rider loved not speed, being made from thee.
  For to do I ensconce me here
  Since, like a sad slave, I will be gone,
  For I must ne'er love him whom thou dost hate.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And I, a tyrant, have no leisure taken.
O, that I have no leisure taken
To any other pass my grief,
And in the onset come;
They are but dressings of a former sight,
Spending again what is already spent.
  For I am still with them,
  And they with thee, I engraft them new.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine own fears are in my mind.
For how do I then fortify
Against the injuries of life that I do?
O, no, I will be cast away
From hands of falsehood, in sure wards of trust!
But when my sleep, in dreams they are but waking no such matter
Of others' voices that my adder's sense
To critic and to flatterer stopped are.
  All men are bad and in this,
  That all the world besides methinks are dead.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And that unfair which fairly doth excel;
For to thy sensual fault I bring in sense--
Thy adverse party is thy advocate--
And 'gainst myself a lawful plea commence.
Such civil war is in my love and hate
  That I an accessary needs must be
  To that sweet thief which sourly robs from me.


