In loving thee thou know'st I am forsworn,
But thou art twice forsworn, to me love swearing;
In act thy bed-vow broke, and new faith torn;
Thou art the tenth muse, ten times more in worth
Than those old nine which rhymers invocate;
And he that calls on thee, let him bring forth
Eternal numbers to outlive long date.
Let him, true in love, but truly write,
And then believe me, my love is as a poet's rage
As any old quill doth come too short,
Spending again what is already spent.
  Then may I dare to speak of that which I think buried.
  For I must ne'er love him whom thou dost hate.


Cupid laid by his brand and fell asleep.
